## 1. Introduction
For metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), the primary requirement for the rapid diagnosis of diseases and the development of therapeutic strategies is the efficient detection of biomarkers (Yang and Yang, 2020; K. Zhang et al., 2020). Moreover, the process of diagnosis of diseases using biomarker detection can now apply the novel MOF-based tech­ niques capable of associating diagnostics and therapeutics for patient treatment at an early stage(Aziz et al., 2020; Mu et al., 2020; C. Wang et al., 2019; Zhu et al., 2019). Human life is based on biofluids and biomolecules and any type of disorder may cause serious diseases such as cancer, heart failure, kidney disorders, and HIV (Aziz et al., 2020; Hirata et al., 2020). Among these, cancer is a life-threatening disease, causing millions of human deaths, owing to its high metastatic fatality rate and diffusion capacity (Jemal et al., 1999; Jo and Ban, 2016). The detection of cancers at different stages delegates the role of biomarker detection. This could be achieved by the use of artificially developed systems capable of detecting cancer biomarkers to provide information about cancer cell growth (Gong et al., 2020; Hassanpour and Dehghani,
2017).
In recent years, various technologies and methods have been developed to detect cancer at different stages via optical and electro­ chemical signal changes, which could help reduce cancer mortality (Wu and Qu, 2015). In the last few decades, enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA)-based methods have been used for clinical diagnosis or detection of cancer biomarkers (Clark and Adams, 1977; Engvall and Perlmann, 1971). On the other side, electrochemical and electro­ chemical impedance spectroscopy-based methods have potentially applied to detect cancer biomarkers for target binding and specific re­ sponses in the redox potential changes (Yang et al., 2014). Also, optical methods have also played significant roles in the detection of cancer biomarkers, owing to their highly sensitive and naked eye-detectable indications (Wang et al., 2010). In addition to this, surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS), electron microscopy, mass spectrometry, and NMR spectroscopy have yielded platforms for the detection of cancer biomarkers (Dong et al., 2020; Rodrigues et al., 2017; Zanjani et al., 2019). Imaging has been used for the last few decades to detect cancers at different stages. The study of full field mammography increased sensitivity through dense tissues (Singh et al., 2008). Despite all these techniques, luminescence and electrochemical-based bio­ sensors are preferred due to their easy handling, low cost, high sensi­ tivity, and rapid signal changes properties. Also, various types of materials, such as molecular ligands, covalent organic frameworks, metal-organic frameworks, nanoparticles (NPs), carbon dots (CDs), carbon nanotubes (CNT), and hydrogel, have been used as cancer-detecting materials by employing these techniques (Ji et al., 2010; M. X. Liu et al., 2020; Nunna et al., 2019; Suzuka et al., 2021; Yan et al., 2019).
MOFs are potential candidates with high regeneration, storage, and separation because of high porosity and the presence of functional sites, which make use of pore space for capturing different species, a tunable framework, and high regeneration capacities. Hence, this sensory area has been explored for the detection of various types of chemical and biochemical species, such as pesticides, nitro explosives, metal ions, anions, gas capturing, small organic molecules and biomolecules, and for real time health monitoring (Fig. 1). (Fu et al., 2018; Goswami et al., 2019; Hu et al., 2020; Yang et al., 2020; X. L. Yang et al., 2021; Zhao et al., 2018). The roles of different functionalities and pore sizes are appreciable for the selective sensing of cancer biomarkers (Peng et al., 2020). Modification via the insertion of active species in MOFs creates efficient and selective binding sites for the guest species. The insertion of different types of active species inside the MOFs led to the formation of fabricated MOFs with enhanced sensing properties(Fu et al., 2021).
Subsequently, the study and development of fabricated MOF sensors for the detection of cancer biomarkers became a crucial research area for public health (D. Liu et al., 2018; Wen et al., 2021; Xian et al., 2020). The insertion of nanoparticles (NPs), inorganic and organic lumnophores and the presence of immobilizing functional groups such as $(-\mathsf{N O}_{2};$ , $\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ $-\mathrm{OH}$ , –COOH, ${\mathrm{-CONH}}_{2}$ , $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , $\mathrm{-F},$ and $\mathbf{-CF_{3}})$ in MOF materials leads to higher electrostatic interactions, H-bonding, and $\pi$ -interactions with the guest molecules useful for various applications (Bai et al., 2020; Mohan et al., 2018a, 2018b; Zhao, 2016). Additionally, the sensing behavior of MOFs toward cancer biomarkers has been studied with the help of various experimental and computational tech­ niques. Luminescence and electrochemical studies of MOF sensors pro­ vided rapid and cost-effective devices for the sensing of cancer biomarkers(Lv et al., 2021). In addition, modeling and computational screening of MOFs are beneficial to perform geometrical analysis for calculating pore size, surface area and porosity, and performing mo­ lecular simulations, for example, in interactions between MOFs and cancer biomarkers in different phases, with respect to potential energy (Centrone et al., 2011). Various types of MOF sensors for the detection of biomarkers with high selectivity and rapid and efficient sensing can be developed with the use of spectroscopic and electrochemical techniques (Xia et al., 2021; Xu et al., 2019).
The fabricated MOFs could potentially be a better sensing tool for the early detection of cancer cells via the detection of cancer biomarkers(Cai et al., 2015). Biomarkers are inclusive molecules, such as exosomes, DNA, genes, proteins and hormones, responsible for providing the in­ formation about human health and have functional sites that can easily bind with MOFs(Du et al., 2020). Moreover, the information associated with cancer biomarkers, such as rearrangements, missing genes, and extra copies of genes, are significantly helpful to develop new MOF sensors as cancer-detecting devices (Qureshi et al., 2012; Tholen et al., 2020). Additionally, MOFs are capable of detecting biomarkers released from cancer cells or tissues; hence, they have been converted into rapid tools to diagnose cancers (Salarian et al. n.d.).
The research area of MOF sensors for the detection of cancer bio­ markers has been explored and studied with the development of multifunctional and fabricated MOFs (J. Liu et al., 2021). It attempts to aid in the detection of cancers at an early stage and diagnosis before imaging (Díaz-Ferna´ndez et al., 2020). A literature study reported by Gui and coworkers summarized the progress on dual-emission ratio-­ metric fluorescence probes for the detection of biomarkers, as catego­ rized based on reference signal changes (Gui et al., 2019). In another review study, Sadighbayan and coworkers summarized the study of the detection of cancerous cell biomarkers related to lung cancer. The bio­ markers detection study covered the need for sensitive and reliable tools for the preclinical diagnosis of lung cancer. The level of biomarkers, such as DNA, proteins, and metabolites, released from cancerous cells revealed the stages of lung cancer (Sadighbayan et al., 2019). In a few other studies, research groups of Li, Chen and Kal-Koshvandi summa­ rized MOF-based sensors for the detection of biomarkers and described luminescence and optical behaviors.(L. Chen et al., 2020; Kal-Kosh­ vandi, 2020; B. Li et al., 2020) Despite these notable reviews, insights into strategies to improve sensing, sensing principles, sensitivity and selectivity, limit of detection (LOD), computational modeling, binding interactions, and articulation of mechanisms have received limited attention. Thus, there is an urgent need to give considerable attention to this research area for in-depth investigation of cancers and their detec­ tion at an early stage.
Herein, we have summarized and discussed the latest fabricated MOF-based luminescent and electrochemical sensors to detect cancer biomarkers due to their advanced signal transduction properties compared to conventional techniques. MOFs as artificial devices for the detection of cancer biomarkers, as well as fabrication strategies to improve the detection potential of MOFs, have been discussed. The study provides an innovative technique to detect the cancer biomarkers by the use of luminescence and electrochemical MOF sensors. The findings such as role of fabricated materials, media and signal trans­ ductions to detect cancer biomarkers have been discussed well. The insights into the electron and energy transfer between MOFs and cancer biomarkers through a function of their molecular orbital positions have been studied. The activation of fabricated MOFs in form of luminescence and an electrochemical platform for sensitive and selective detection of biomarkers is described well. In each example the active role of func­ tional sites and NPs in MOFs for information about luminescence and electrochemical signal transductions is discussed for proposed sensing mechanisms. Through this study, we have presented insights and detailed discussions related to luminescence and electrochemical fabri­ cated MOF sensors and the associated binding modes, electron and en­ ergy transfer, sensing mechanisms, signal transductions, modeling, and LOD. In addition, key factors for enhancing the detection potential and major challenges are discussed. This review would be beneficial a col­ lective overview of the basics, logical order, with the intent to further the development of new cancer-detecting devices.
## 2. Advantage of MOF-based artificial devices in cancer biomarkers detection
The detection of cancer biomarkers could be helpful for the screening and diagnosis, examine the efficacy of cancer treatment. Developing portable materials with low-cost and high sensitivity for detecting can­ cers is important. The combination of artificial materials, cancer bio­ markers, and enhanced techniques have resulted in better artificial devices for the detection of cancers (R. Liu et al., 2020). These studies involve the dynamics of the sensor, level of expression, modification, and interactions with biomarkers (Van Gool et al., 2020). This collected information is significantly applicable in the selection of material de­ vices for the detection of cancer biomarkers. Although different types of biomarkers are present for the recognition of various diseases, we will focus on cancer biomarkers that could be helpful in the detection of
cancers (Table 1). (Fan et al., 2012; Kimura et al., 2017; Wei et al., 2018)
To diagnose diseases, many molecular sensors and materials have been developed and reported in the literature (Altintas et al., 2014; Chang et al., 2019; Liden et al., 2008; Tricoli and Neri, 2018). But MOF sensors showed precise results with low-cost and high efficiency for evaluating artificial intelligence systems for clinical use (Bi et al., 2019). With the development of multifunctional MOFs relies on the develop­ ment of sensors for biomarkers with rapid sensing, reusability, and cost-effective properties (S. Zhang et al., 2020). Hence, fabricated MOFs with various functional sites proved to be better devices for clinical application as compare to the conventional sensors, with respect to the diagnosis of cancers in tissue samples (Qi et al., 2017). MOFs showed potential outcomes and were more helpful for clinical pathology di­ agnoses and patient safety (Raab and Grzybicki, 2010; Zhu et al., 2018). So, the use of MOFs in artificial intelligence models for early diagnosis of cancer cellscould possibly be explored (Marya et al., 2020). However, other techniques based on radio therapies and imaging frequently depend on visual evaluation during treatment. Although, an artificial intelligence-based system could be facilitated via the diagnosis of bio­ markers using MOFs in clinical applications (Bernini et al., 2014). In particular, an artificial intelligence system, based on sensing bio­ markers, has the potential to improve qualitative understanding of cancer detection via the development of new sensors (Murakami et al., 2020). Notably, crystalline MOFs with better morphology and structural properties became recognition tools for biomarkers. The structural properties, morphology, and modeling information of MOFs can be used to predict the recognition behavior of guest biomolecules or biomarkers (Dantas and Neimark, 2020; Vazhappilly et al., 2016).
Recently, Yue and coworkers developed Zn(II)-MOF as a biosensor. The unique structural properties of MOFs, in the presence of space groups in crystalline Zn(II)-MOFs, helped to identify the asymmetric unit of MOFs with $\scriptstyle2\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ion. Moreover, the combination of computa­ tional and experimental studies provided deep insight into mechanistic information about the lower energy of the lower unoccupied molecular orbitals (LUMOs) of the ligand, compared to that of the analyte (Yue et al., 2018). Also, the use of modeling studies is helpful to explain the structural optimization of MOFs and MOF-analyte binding structures (Azar et al., 2020). Hence, porous material-based artificial models contribute to biomarker diagnostics with advancements such as rapid processing, small sample volume, and short analysis times (Ko et al., 2008). Although no single material or technique can efficiently detect the different biomarkers within the same sample, the electrochemicaland luminescence-based fabricated MOF sensors showed better mea­ surement in the detection of cancer biomarkers, as discussed in this review. Therefore, the arrangement of different sensors is necessary to measure low levels of cancer biomarkers for their rapid detection, based on various spectroscopic, spectrometric, and electrochemical techniques (Table 2) (Chinen et al., 2015; Gholizadeh et al., 2020; Hu et al., 2010; Matta et al., 2010; Raja et al., 2020; Ren et al., 2020).
## 2.1. Fabrication strategies to improve the detection potential of MOFs
The fabrication of MOFs is responsible for the enhanced properties such as active binding sites, surface area and encapsulation into pores. Another fabrication of MOFs decides the applications of MOF based on incorporated functionalities and active site. For the fabrication of MOFs different strategies such as metal-node as active sites, incorporation and encapsulation of biomimetic, encapsulation, bottle-around-ship strat­ egy, De novo assembly strategy, MOF on MOF, and post-synthetic modification strategy have been reported (Fig. 2). (Chang et al., 2020; Chen and Wu, 2019; Ikigaki et al., 2019; Sun et al., 2021; Y. Zhou et al., 2020) In addition, rationalizing MOF crystals into the nanoregime provided a better approach and enhanced properties. This can be ach­ ieved by choosing proper organic ligand, setting suitable parameters of time, temperature and heating rate. Moreover, modification of surface by changing ligand and controlled crystal growth allowed to tailor features of MOF sensors for better morphology, pore, and size, for contextual sensing applications (Aldren Usman et al., 2020.). The fundamental parameters describing electronic properties of MOFs could be modified by metal incorporation, node replacement or extension, and metal-ligand coordination. The selection of edge in MOF sensors is important for the modification and changes in the density of electronic states. This edge selection demonstrates the tailoring of MOF electronic properties with topology and node engineering, without affecting the porosity and structural integrity (Dolgopolova et al., 2017).
The insertion of Au NPs in 2,2-bipyridyl)-ruthenium(II) [Ru $\left(\mathrm{bpy}_{3}^{2+}\right]$ linked $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF provided better octahedral morphology and binding sites for the detection of amyloid- $\cdot\upbeta$ (Aβ) protein. The fabricated MOF showed Zn, Ru, C and O as the major elements that justify the encapsulation of $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)_{3}}}^{2+}$ in Zn-MOFs (Zhao et al., 2019). The insertion of NPs in $z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF can increase the surface area and function­ alization with folic acid molecules responsible for the production of sensitive materials that act as a sensor for FAR-rich cancer cells (e.g.
HeLa , H1299 cells and HEK-293 cells)(Du et al., 2019). Moreover, the fabrication of biosensors for the detection of cancer biomarkers has been carried out via the simple addition of nanoparticles followed by the joining of two separate DNAs by specific processes (C. Wang et al., 2020). In another study, Xiang and coworkers designed wrapped MOF sensors for microRNA (miRNA) on the microorganism surface with better bar coding ability due to their different morphologies and sizes. This type of fabrication resulted in enhanced properties such as stable rigid shell, large specific surface area and metal ions with multiple binding sites (Xiang et al., 2020).
Recently, an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA)-fabri­ cated MOF was reported by Zhand and coworkers; it acted as a sensor for the selective detection of programmed cell death ligand 1 (PD-L1) pro­ tein, a cancer biomarker. First, an ELISA plate was coated with a layer of polydopamine-polyethyleneimine (PDA PEI) and precursor solution preparation (zinc nitrate and 2-methyl imidazole). Then, the PD-L1 antibodies were added into the PDA PEI-coated wells for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ and coated with $\mathrm{\Au/Pd}$ nanoparticles (Zhand et al., 2020). In fact, the insertion of external particles in MOFs could be responsible for providing a large surface area and abundant active sites for the devel­ opment of a better sensor (Hu et al., 2020). Furthermore, the fabrication of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF with carbon nanotubes (CNT) resulted in a highly crystalline structure with an enhanced capability of producing high electro­ chemical signals for the detection of cancer biomarker Carbohydrate antigen 125 (CA 125) (Biswas et al., 2021). As compared to the non-fabricated MOFs, the fabricated MOFs were more suitable for insertion and showed better luminescence and electrical conductivity (Fig. 2).(Dong et al., 2018)
2.2. Sensing principles and common mechanisms of MOF sensors for the detection of biomarkers
The fabricated MOFs can have multifunctional electron-rich and deficient functionalities. The insertion of NPs, fluorophores and elec­ troactive materials made the MOFs precise devices for the detection of cancer biomarkers. For biomarker sensing using fabricated MOFs, the basic principle of sensing is based on the chemical changes that are intended in the form of optical and electric signals. These changes can be easily observed using techniques such as UV–visible, fluorescence spectroscopic and cyclic voltammetric techniques (Afreen et al., 2020). Different types of noncovalent interactions are responsible for these signal changes, upon sensing of cancer biomarkers using MOF materials. For the sensing of biomarkers, various techniques could operate based on mass, charge, electron, energy and signal changes, among others (Chang et al., 2019; C. Chen et al., 2020). However, we focused on the factors responsible for signal transduction during luminescence and electrochemical sensing of cancer biomarkers.
The principle of MOF luminescence sensing of cancer biomarkers is based on a change in the chemical transduction in the form of optical signals in their spectra (Shen et al., 2020). Moreover, it could be observed directly via a change in shape and structure of the observed spectra. The changes in signals, observed during biomarker sensing, significantly determine the efficiency of the sensor. However, to gain an in-depth understanding about binding behavior and mechanism of MOF sensors, with respect to biomarkers, the study of electron transfer (ET) and energy transfer (EnT) between higher occupied molecular orbitals (HOMOs) and LUMOs are necessary (Fan et al., 2020). Data about the transfer of charge and energy from molecular orbitals is helpful to explain the mechanisms and types of interactions between MOFs and biomarkers. Upon photons being excited at a particular wavelength, the emission spectra are observed, which could involve one or more than one transition stage. When a particular analyte interacts with MOFs, then the consequent changes in the energy stages are responsible for the changes observed in the spectra (Mohan et al., 2021).
The electrochemical detection of cancer biomarkers is the result of translating chemical signals into electrical signals (Khanmohammadi et al., 2020). The study of these changes is helpful to explain the sensing behavior of electrochemical sensors for various biomarkers. Fabricated MOFs that interact with different nanoparticles and biomolecules worked as a gatekeeper to prevent the electroactive compounds. This is helpful to enhance the stability and sensing properties of the function­ alized MOFs. The interactions between MOFs and fabricated functional sites resulted in the cleavage reaction responsible for the formation of an electroactive compound in the solution and two strong electrochemical signals (Sinha et al., 2019). In addition, the roles of metal ions and oxidation states also affect the electrochemical sensing of cancer bio­ markers. Moreover, the conducting and nonconducting nature of bio­ markers is responsible for the electrochemical signal changes. In some cases, conformational changes in the fabricated materials can lead to better sensing of biomarkers. Moreover, the surface of MOFs is a dependable platform for the ET process during electrochemical sensing of biomarkers. In some cases, the formation of G-quadruplex between the fabricated materials and analyte is responsible for electrochemical sensing. Hence, the electrochemical signals observed can be explained by the ET process between the active surface and analyte. The fabricated MOF materials owing to the potential electrodes, and drop-coated with nanoparticles are accurate platform for the detection of cancer bio­ markers (Prasad et al., 2020).
To study the sensing mechanisms of fabricated MOF sensors, the effect of different inserted NPs and their luminescence and electro­ chemical redox activity was investigated by changes in the signals and nature of spectra observed. For example, the Cu-MOF showed redox process after the insertion of Au particles with enhanced the conduc­ tivity to a great extent and was utilized to detect low levels of dopamine. The enhanced activity of MOF could be due to the excellent ET and the synergistic effect for the oxidation of dopamine and NPs (Qiu et al., 2019). For luminescence sensing of cancer biomarkers, the ET and EnT mechanisms depend on the molecular orbital energies of analytes and MOFs. In case the MOF ligand had higher energy than the analyte, it could support the ET from ligand to analyte, which is responsible for luminescence quenching. Hence, based on the shapes and relative orbital energies of the HOMOs and LUMOs of the ligands and analytes, the main mechanisms for signal transductions in electrochemical and luminescence sensing can be explained (Wu et al., 2018).Generally, the signal changes involve photoinduced electron transfer (PET), fluores­ cence resonance energy transfer (FRET), resonance energy transfer (RET), charge transfer (CT), electron transfer (ET), energy transfer (EnT), inner filter effect (IFE), internal system crossing (ISC) and che­ lation enhanced fluorescence (CHEF). All the mechanistic principles of sensing are a consequenceof chemical and structural changes that could be observed in the form of optical or electrochemical signals, owing to the energy, electron, and charge transfer strategy employed (Fig. 3). (Mendes et al., 2020; Yan, 2021)
The presence of active chromophores in MOFs could be responsible for enhanced optical signals. The EnT and CT can be understood by in­ sights associated with the structural, electronic, and chemical diversities of MOFs. The EnT and CT transfer system in 1,3,6,8-tetrakis(p-benzoica­ cid)pyrene (H4TBAPy)-linked $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF were found to depend on the excitation wavelengths, i.e., CT at $460~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , while at $670\ \mathrm{nm,EnT}$ was observed (Li et al., 2019). Although the EnT processes between donor and acceptor in MOFs is the function of the molecular orbitals of ligand and analytes. In some cases, the rational mechanisms between multiple emitting centers help explain the EnT processes (W. Cao et al., 2020). The fabricated MOFs are able to photoreduce redox proteins via ET due to the presence of NPs. The presence of NPs could be responsible for RET. Hence, the complex formation tendency of inserted NPs in MOFs and bioanalytes promoted more than one mechanism. The ISC system in MOF sensors could be helpful to study the sensing mechanisms with many degrees of freedom in the non adiabatic and spin-orbit couplings associated (Mai et al., 2015).
## 3. MOF sensors for cancer biomarkers
## 3.1. Luminescence MOF sensors for detection of cancer biomarkers
Fabricated luminescent MOFs have been potentially applied as a tool for the detection of biomarkers in human serum. Recently, Wang and coworkers reported aptamer-functionalized 2-aminoterephthalic acidlinked magnetic MOF nanoprobe for the rapid recognition of bio­ markers in human serum (Z. Wang et al., 2019). In another example, the 2-amino-1, 4-benzenedicarboxylic acid-linked and ascorbic acid induced Fe-MOF acted as a fluorescence sensor with a color change due to CT from the oxidation inhibition of a chromogenic substrate $^{3,3^{\prime},5}$ , ${\boldsymbol{5}}^{\prime}$ -tetramethylbenzidine. The functionalization of Fe-MOF with ascorbic acid enhanced the sensing properties and acted as a qualitative evalu­ ator for cancer biomarker Prostate specific antigen (PSA), with a LOD of $180~\mathrm{pg/mL}$ (Fig. 4a).(Xie et al., 2020) The fabrication of DNA on MOFs has already attracted attention, with respect to bioanalytical applica­ tions. DNA on the surface of MOF can easily detect analytes, and the presence of different functional groups could explain the sensing mechanism and possible noncovalent interactions (S. Wang et al., 2020).
Meng and coworkers designed a DNA@Cu-MOF nanosystem for the fluorescence detection of miRNAs. The utility of DNAzymeas nanop­ robes and activation and release of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , a DNAzyme precursor, helped to detect fluorescence signals for the detection of miRNA (X. Meng et al., 2020). Recently, Afzalinia and Mirzaee reported a 2-aminoterephthalic acid-linked La(III)-(MOF)- $\cdot\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}@\mathbf{NP}$ , which acted as a selective and sen­ sitive biosensor for miRNA-155 biomarker by the FRET strategy of sensing mechanism through EnT from ${\boldsymbol{5}}^{\prime}$ -amino-labeled surfaces; moreover, the LOD determined was 5.5 fM (Afzalinia and Mirzaee, 2020). In another study, Zhu et al. reported a hybridization strategy based on DNA and $\mathbf{N},\mathbf{N}^{\prime}$ -bis-(2-hydroxyethyl)dithiooxamidato linked MOFs that potentially acted as biomarker sensors and followed the FRET mechanism. The insertion of DNA could be responsible for better bind­ ing surface, as well as for the luminescence properties of MOF, upon binding of analytes (Zhu et al., 2013).
In FRET, the small binding length $\left(<10\mathrm{nm}\right)$ fluorophore with sensor could be responsible for nonradiative EnT. In most cases, it was observed that the insertion of DNA enhanced sensor properties to detect bio­ markers (Dong et al., 2021). In addition, the development of FRET-based sensors could minimize the effects of system-related in consistencies and avoid false-positive signals. This could be achieved via the addition of DNA to the developed sensor for ensuring high FRET efficiency (He et al., 2017). The signal changes process of luminescence MOF sensors for cancer biomarkers detection involved the activation of MOFs and the detection result in signal changes (Fig. 4b). Recently, the 2,3,5,6-tetrakis (3,6-bis(4-carboxyphenyl)-9H-carbazol-9-yl)terephthalic acid linked MOF, designed by Kong and coworkers, had enhanced luminescence properties with advancement in structure, including positive surface potential and modified capsule-like morphology. The presence of 8-con­ nected $\boldsymbol{z}\mathrm{r(VI)}$ clusters enabled detection of cytoplasmic biomarker microRNA-21, a cancer biomarker, via a change in fluorescence spectra, even in real samples. Ratiometric changes in the fluorescence spectra could be due to the electrostatic and noncovalent interactions involved in electron and charge transfer (Kong et al., 2020).
The fabricated MOF sensors have been applied for the detection of DNA, which is a hereditary material and biological information carrier. Recently, Tan and coworkers prepared hybrid MOFs using magnetic porous carbon (MPC) nanocomposite, which acted as a turn-offsensor for single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) with a LOD of 1 nM. Quenching in fluorescence spectra could be due to a significant involvement of elec­ trostatic interactions between the MPC and MOF (Tan et al., 2016). Another fabricated MOF sensor for biothiols was reported by Jiang and coworkers. The oxidase mimic fabricated and peroxidase substrate $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ , $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine-based p-phthalic acidlinked MOF MIL-53(Fe) acted as a potential colorimetric sensor for the detection of biothiols, with a LOD of $120~\mathrm{{nM}}$ . Notably, the model worked as an effective tool for the detection of cancer cells and identified cellular biothiols in normal (RWPE-1) and cancer (LNCap) cell lines (Jiang et al., 2019).
Han and coworkers developed a luminol– $\mathrm{{\cdotH_{2}O_{2}}}$ –MOF sensing plat­ form to detect the a-fetoprotein biomarker for hepatocellular cancer, with a LOD of $7.7\times10^{-11}\mathrm{g/L}$ . The positive charged environment of the MOF and the negative charge of the aptamer enable the detection of cancer cells through electrostatic interactions responsible for the signal transductions and spectra changes (Han et al., 2020). In another example, the emission spectra of AuNPs inserted Tb-MOFs was restored with the introduction of tumor biomarker PSA, and a linear range of $\scriptstyle1-100\ {\mathrm{ng/mL}}$ with a LOD of $0.36~\mathrm{ng/mL}$ . The model was found appli cable for the detection of cancer biomarker PSA in human urine samples. The mechanism of sensing was based on the off-on process via the AuNPs and interaction of PSA with $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{NPs@MOF}}$ (Fig. 5a and Fig. 5b). (Qu et al., 2019) In another example, the luminescence spectra of 2,6-naphthalene dicarboxylic linked Mn(II)-MOF got enhanced with the addition of biothiols biomarkers (Bej et al., 2020). In addition, the fabricated nanomagnetic 5-aminoisophthalic acid linked Cu(II)-MOF showed strong emission behavior and could detect alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) selectively in concentration range $1.0{-}520~\mathrm{ng/mL}$ with a LOD of 1.18 $\mathrm{ng/mL}$ and quantitation limit of $3.58~\mathrm{ng/mL}$ (Sheta et al., 2019). In another example, lanthanide MOF acted as a sensor for biomarkers for carcinoid tumor serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine) and metabolite (5-hydroxyindole-3-acetic acid), as a result of quenching in lumines­ cence spectra upon the addition of serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine, LOD $0.66\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~m})$ and metabolite (5-hydroxyindole-3-acetic acid, LOD $0.54\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M})$ , with a fast response (within $1\ \mathrm{min}\cdot$ )(Wu et al., 2018). Therefore, the developed non-destructive diagnostic methods of cancers are important due to their rapidness and cost-effectiveness (Shang et al., 2017).
The detection efficiency of a sensor can depend on the metal centers and inserted materials. For example, the presence of Ln(III) ions in ZnMOFs enhanced the MOF sensing properties toward the detection of lysophosphatidic acid owned the metal center effect that could be responsible for the EnT and antenna effect (Zhang et al., 2015). Notably, the quenching effects on MOFs luminescence spectra could be explained according to PET. The higher energy level of LUMO of ligands/MOFs than that of biomarkers supports the ET from MOF to analyte, which is responsible for the quenching effect. Also the lower energy of LUMO of ligand/MOF than that of the analyte could be responsible for the absence of PET process for luminescence quenching (Ying et al., 2021). MOF luminescence sensors for the detection of cancer biomarkers are pre­ sented in tabular form in Table 3.
The nature of metal ions, ligands, and fabricated materials helps understand the luminescence spectra changes and mechanistic behavior exhibited by MOF sensors. Recently, Hao and Li designed a fabricated luminescent MOF using adenine; the designed MOF (Eu-adenine-MOF) was a good sensor for 8-oxo- $2^{\prime}$ -deoxyguanosine (8-oxo-dG), a cancer biomarker. The fabricated MOF exhibited a strong affinity to form multiple H-bonds with 8-oxo-dG. The presence of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions in MOFswas responsible for the ligand-to-metal EnT (Hao and Li, 2019). Moreover, the antenna effect due to the presence of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ could not be ignored; hence, the presence of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ ions in MOFs is considered to enhance sensing properties (Schibel et al., 2011). MOFs with lanthanide metal ions showed transitions from d-orbitals to f-orbitals and were respon­ sible for the EnT process (Bünzli and Eliseeva, 2010). More insights in the mechanistic information can be collected via modeling studies; the noncovalent and H-bonding interactions between MOFs and biomarkers can potentially inhibit the rotations and shorten the lifetime, resulting in enhancement of luminescence spectra. In such a process, only PET process cannot be sufficient to explain the luminescence mechanism. EnT and CT could also be involved, owing to the shape changes of luminescence spectra (Bünzli, 2010). In MOF sensors comprising a flu­ orophore with LUMO of higher energy than that of analyte’s LUMO, PET could be responsible for the quenching process (Gao et al., 2017). The sensing mechanism of MOF sensors can be explained based on the intercrossing system and EnT process at different exciting wavelengths. For example, Eu-adenine-MOF shows a dual response at $295\mathrm{nm}$ (inhi­ bition of ISC and EnT) and ${309}\mathrm{nm}$ (activation of ISC and EnT), but the quenching process dominates and can be identified by the naked eye under a UV lamp (Fig. 6).(Hao and Li, 2019)
## 3.2. Electrochemical MOF sensors for detection of cancer biomarkers
MOFs have attracted much attention as electrochemical sensors due to the presence of redox and catalytic active sites. The presence of active metal ions, ligands and linkers fulfill the functions required for elec­ trochemical sensing.(L. Liu et al., 2018) The use of electrochemical techniques potentially meet the increasing demand for accurate diag­ nosis of cancers at an early stage. MOFs have been fabricated and immobilized by changing in synthetic methods to have high sensitivity and enhanced recognition potential for cancer biomarkers (H.-S. Wang et al., 2020).
For example, the insertion of PtCu NPs in Cu-MOF provided the active centers for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as electroconductive support for nonenzymatic electrochemical sensor. The synergy between MOF and NPs enhanced the activity of the sensor toward the detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in living tumor cells as well (Chen et al., 2018). Recently, Singh and coworkers designed benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate linked Co-MOF for the recognition of vascular endothelial growth factor 165 (VEGF165), a quintessential biomarker in cancers. The changes in redox potentials in the presence of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{IFe}}(\mathbf{CN})6]^{3-/4-}$ could be observed as a consequence of charge interactions between VEGF165 and MOF, with a LOD of ${\mathsf{5.23}}{\mathsf{p M}}$ , under real condition of pH 7. The change in electrochemical signals could be due to the reduction process, owing to the non-conducting nature of the antigen (Singh et al., 2020). In another study, Ou and coworkers designed an electrochemical sensor by immobolization of aptamers. Pt NPs inserted MOF PCN-224 and G-quadruplex linked dual-aptamer were fixed on gold electrodes through Au-S bonds for cancer cell detection. The presence of Au NPs and aptamer surfaces provided the active sites involved in ET, for the detection of cancer biomarker (Ou et al., 2019)
Notably, the metal centers and inserted metal NPs in electrochemical MOF sensors acted to provide the active sites surface to detect cancer biomarkers. Also, it could be helpful to generate electrochemical signals involving redox process through the different oxidation states of metals and electron-rich and deficient functionalities of biomarkers (Dai et al., 2019). Recently, Dai and coworkers developed a sandwich-type Co-MOF as an electrochemical immunosensor for PSA detection. The MOF was fabricated with Pd NPs and amino-zeolitic imidazolate framework-67 (Pd/NH2-ZIF-67). It behaved as a novel redox mediator for the detec­ tion of cancer biomarkers in a broad detection range, from $100\mathrm{fg/mL}$ to $\mathrm{50~ng/mL}$ , with a LOD of $0.03\mathrm{\pg/mL}$ (Dai et al., 2019). In another example, Hatami and coworkers designed electrochemical aptasensor fabricated withgraphene oxide nanocomposite on $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ -MOF, that utilized for the selective detection of MUC1, even in the presence of various amino acids and proteins. Further, the proposed mechanism for the MOF sensor involved a decrease in redox peak $[\mathsf{C u(I I)-C u(I)}]$ in MOF due to formation of MUC1-aptamer complex (Hatami et al., 2019).
In another study, Qiu and coworkers prepared a tetrachloroauric acid linked and gold NPs inserted $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ -based MOF composite material that acted as a sensitive electrochemical ratiometric sensor for the detection of dopamine. The presence of gold NPs and poly (xanthurenic acid) on the Cu-tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin improved the electrocatalytic performance of the Cu-MOF for cancer biomarkers detection by the dopamine oxidation on the surface of the MOF (Qiu et al., 2019). Recently, Sun and coworkers designed a hybrid platform 2-methylimidazole linked MOF for exosomes cancer biomakers detec­ tion by the combination of black phosphorus nanosheets and ferrocene doped MOFs on indium tin oxide (ITO) slices and methylene blue (MB)-labeled single-strand DNA aptamer on the ITO slice. The presence of MB resulted in the reduction of redox current and ET that enhanced signal transduction for exosomes detection with a LOD of 100 parti­ cles/mL (Sun et al., 2020). In another example, Hu and coworkers designed a 2-aminoterephthalic acid linked MOF fabricated with Au NPs and mercaptosuccinic acid. The fabricated MOF had hydrophilic and magnetic properties and acted as a sensor for glycopeptide a breast cancer biomarker from standard protein digests with a sensitivity of 0.5 $\mathrm{\fmol/\upmuL}$ (Hu et al., 2019). Furthermore, the utility of 2-amino-1, 4-phthalic acid linked UiO-66-NH2 MOF with ruthenium metal was explored to detect the CA 15–3 antibody as a biomarker. The presence of amide functionalities in the MOF enhances the capability for binding with CA 15–3, with a LOD of1. $.7705\times10^{-5}\mathrm{U}/\mathrm{mL}$ (Xiong et al., 2019).
MOF materials synthesized by using MOF on MOF strategy have high tunable properties and showed precise sensing properties for cancer biomarkers. Recently, Zhou and coworkers designed new fabricated bimetallic MOF architectures that acted as aptasensor scaffolds for detecting cancer markers protein tyrosine kinase-7 (PTK7). The Zr-MOFon-Zn-MOF demonstrates a multilayered nanosheet structure, while the Zn-MOF on $z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF hybrid exhibits a hierarchically decussated foliace. Electrochemical studies revealed the behavior of synthesized hybrid MOFs, wherein $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF facilitated the binding of the aptamer and ZnMOF stabilized the $\mathsf{\Delta}_{\mathsf{G}}$ -quadruplex formed by aptamer strands and PTK7, with a LOD of 0.66 and $0.84~\mathrm{pg/mL}$ , respectively (Fig. 7). (Zhou et al., 2019) In another example, Wang and coworkers designed two different bimetallic TbFe-MOFs by combining MOFs. The designed hybrid MOF acted as a good electrochemical sensor for the carbohydrate antigen 125 (CA125). The Tb-MOF-on-Fe-MOF exhibited enhanced combining properties as G-quadruplex between aptamer and CA125 could be due to better immobilization behavior with an extreme LOD of $58~\upmu\mathrm{U/mL}$ towards CA125, hence a potential tool for early diagnosis of tumors (M. Wang et al., 2019).
MOF sensor for the electrochemical detection of PD-L1þ exosomes, with high sensitivity and a detection limit of 334 particles/mL, was re­ ported by Cao and coworkers. The anti-CD63 functionalities with magnetic beads in MOF helped to capture PD-L1þ exosomes through the anti-PD-L1-linked binding probe. The designed MOF materials are with advancement to elevate the level of circulating PD-L1þ exosomes in the real serum samples from patients with metastatic breast cancer (Y. Cao et al., 2020). The fabrication of 2D MOFs and prussian blue analogues (PBAs) produced advanced materials that could overcome the poor electronic conductivity, as well as slow diffusion of MOFs for electrochemical applications. They functioned effectively by reducing transfer resistance and enhancing electron and mass-transfer efficiency. The fabricated electrodes behaved as multifunctional sensors with high sensitivity for glucose and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Additionally, the fabricated MOF was able to detect glucose in human serum, which is representative of its applications in clinical diagnosis (C. Chen et al., 2020).
The immobilization of clusters and the insertion of other particles in the MOFs are responsible for better electrochemical and luminescence properties of MOFs (He et al., 2020). For example, the insertion of the ruthenium complex on the $\boldsymbol{z}\mathrm{r(VI)}$ cluster of PCN-777 in MOF enhanced the electrochemical properties of MOF for the detection of biomarker MUC1. The presence of metal ions on the cluster surface could be responsible for the redox process occurring for the detection of MUC1 (Hu et al., 2018). Furthermore, the DNA amplified MOF was reported by Meng and coworkers; it was applied for the detection and imaging of multiple intracellular mRNAs. The amplification of DNA in MOF and the presence of phosphate ions enhanced the properties such as enzymatic stability and cellular uptake and ensured good biocompatibility. The designed materials efficiently detected TK1 and survivin mRNA, and hence, this strategy could turn into a powerful tool for sensing and detecting cancers at early stages (H. M. Meng et al., 2020). The presence of DNA on the electrodes laid the foundation to detect cancer biomarker miRNAs in the dynamic range from $10\mathrm{aM}$ to 1 fM, with a LOD of 2 aM. The DNA-based sensors follow four-way electrochemical sensing to detect the miRNA. This could be due to the indirect binding of the probe to the analyte miRNA and the involvement of analyte-binding arms of the adaptor strands. (Labib et al., 2013). Li and coworkers designed 2-fluorobenzoic acid and naphthalene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid linked ul­ trasmall (Ag, Cu) metal NPs encapsulated with electrochemically reduced graphene oxide $\operatorname{NPs}@\operatorname{Y}$ -MOF/ERGO that utilized for electro­ chemical detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from cancer cells. The designed MOF ma­ terials showed excellent detection potential with different electron transfer rate constants. The observed linear range for AgNPs encapsu­ lated MOF is $4\mathrm{-}11,000~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ with LOD of $0.18~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . The MOFs electro­ chemical detection of cancer biomarkers could be activated by the introduction of stimulators (Fig. 9).(Li et al., 2018) MOF hybrid material electrochemical sensors for the detection of cancer biomarkers have been summarized in Table 4.
## 4. Important key factors and opportunities for fabricated MOF sensors
The MOF sensors for cancer biomarkers have the potential to be applied in medical science. In this section, we will discussed the key factors and important issues related to detection, stability, surface ac­ tivity, electronic transitions, signal transduction, and reusability, with the intent to provide a detailed summary of relevant literature to enable breakthroughs in the development of new clinical therapies for cancer diagnosis. Fabricated MOFs can solve many problems at the laboratory, as well as at industrial, level. Although not easy, insights into these fabricated MOF sensors may lead to solutions for issues related to rapidness, reusability, and cost-effectiveness in the detection of cancers at an early stage. Further research will provide better opportunities and help create a foundation for the development of new cancer detecting devices at a low cost. It will not only be cost-effective, but will also help save lives of patients. The tailored properties and functionalities in fabricated MOFs are associated with their structures, geometries, and compositions (Z. Wang et al., 2020). Therefore, this area of study is increasingly in focus for the development of new devices. Precise ca­ pacity in sensing biomarkers at the micro level has offered a robust and versatile platform for designing new devices to detect cancer at an early stage (Hu et al., 2021).
## 4.1. Real samples analysis
MOF biosensors for real samples analysis have focused on developing a easy and portable method for rapid screening and early diagnosis of cancer. The utility of MOF biosensors is based on advancement of saliva, blood serum and other body fluids sample collections and their analysis. MOFs have been utilized for direct analysis of cancer biomarkers from real samples with lower costs (Afreen et al., 2020). The detection of cancer biomarers in real samples using MOFs is vital for direct clinical applications. For example, Kong and coworkers used $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF for detection of cytoplasmic biomarker microRNA-21 samples (Kong et al., 2020). In another example ZrHf-MOF utilized to detect HER2 cancer biomarkers from real samples (Gu et al., 2019). Recently, Hu and co­ workers reported the real sample analysis of glycopeptide from serum by the use of fabricated magnetic MOF (Hu et al., 2019). In this way, MOFs provided state-of-the-art methods for early stage cancer biomarkers detection in real-time capture.
## 4.2. Rapid and selective detection of biomarkers
One of the advantages of fabricated MOFs is the rapid and selective detection of cancer biomarkers, as compared to nonfabricated MOFs. Parameters including specific, rapid, and cost-effective detection of biomarkers with low detection limit play crucial roles in the enhancement of MOF applications. The presence of different function­ alities, NPs, metal nodes, and aptamer frameworks of MOFs have pro­ vided an active and better platform, useful for improving the sensing capability (Perera et al., 2021). This could be because fabricated mate­ rial provides a suitable environment for binding and sensing activity, which can be further explored for various applications. The study of fabricated MOF sensors can aid the development of rapid and cost-effective sensors. MOF sensors for biomarkers and the observation of signal or color changes could be a rapid tool for clinical applications (Ebigbo et al., 2020).
## 4.3. Stability of MOF sensors
Another key factor is the stability of fabricated MOFs in various solvents and pH. To develop better MOF sensors for cancer biomarkers, chemical, water, pH, thermal, hydrothermal and mechanical stabilities of fabricated MOFs are necessary. It has been observed that fabricated MOFs have better stabilities, as compared to nonfabricated 3D porous materials. In this section, we will discuss these stabilities and key factors for enhancing the sensing properties of MOF sensors.
## 4.3.1. Water stability
The MOF stability in water can be based on node and linker natures. The adsorption nature of MOFs also affects water stability. It could be measured by the shape of an adsorption isotherm that can explain the interactions between MOF surface and analytes (Burtch et al., 2014; Goswami et al., 2019). It can be examined by immersing the MOFs in water for different time periods, from minutes to days, at room tem­ perature. For example Cu@MIL-91(Al:Eu) MOF was examined for water stability by immersing in water for three days. Then, the luminescence spectra were observed; the MOF acted as a good sensor for uric acid and diagnosis of hyperuricemia (Lian and Yan, 2017). Although in MOF structures, the nodes, metal linker bonds with hydrolysis can form protonated and water hydroxide nodes responsible for weakening the MOF stability (Howarth et al., 2016). Additionally, the presence of stronger bonds in the framework could be responsible for better water stability of MOFs, owing to the rapid involvement of non-porous amorphous materials (Schaate et al., 2011).
## 4.3.2. pH stability
Another key factor is the pH stability of fabricated MOFs, wherein the structural stability and presence of multifunctional sites in fabri­ cated MOFs would help construct potential sensors at optimum pH. Although there is no standard method to evaluate the pH stability of MOFs, it could be evaluated via powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) by immersing the MOFs in different pH solutions. Fabricated MOFs are considered more stable due to the presence of various multifunctional nodes. For example, Zn-MOF with MoS2 NPs inserted behaved as a good sensor for the detection of carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) at $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ , with a LOD of $1.30~\mathrm{pg/mL}$ in a linear range from $2.0\ \mathrm{pg/mL}$ to $10.0\mathrm{ng/}$ mL (Wei et al., 2019). Appropriate modification in physical and chem­ ical properties and the insertion of NPs in the sensor ensured $\mathsf{p H}$ stable sensing of miRNA-155,with LOD 3 aM in the range 0.01 fM to $0.01\ \mathrm{nM}$ (Jiao et al., 2020). The presence of ligands in dicarboxyphenylpyridine-2,6-dicarboxylic acid MOFs improved pH stability between $\mathsf{p H3.90}$ to 7.50. Modeling studies have demonstrated the deprotonation mechanism of uncoordinated carboxyl groups in li­ gands with triplet excited energy level of ligand and transfer of energy from this excited state to metal ions (Xia et al., 2017). Hence, the pH stability of MOF sensors could be enhanced by the selection of appro­ priate ligand, metal ions, and inserted materials.
## 4.3.3. Thermal and mechanical stabilities
Thermal stability of MOFs depends on the breakage of the nodelinker bond with temperature; a higher the bond strength would lead to a higher thermal stability of the MOFs. Thermally stable MOFs could help develop new sensors for operation at different temperatures. For example, L-glutamic acid functionalized Ni, Co and Cu MOFs are ther­ mally stable and showed enhanced sensory responses to analytes (Can et al., 2020). Additionally, metal nodes have been observed to affect thermal stabilities. Cr-MIL-88B–NH2 MOF exhibited better stability than Fe-MIL-88B–NH2 MOF due to the temperature-dependent morphological change, directly in the gas phase (Wang et al., 2018). In addition, cross-linking and catenation in the MOFs can enhance stability through the MOF’s framework interactions.
The presence of excessive porosity in MOFs can be responsible for decreases in mechanical stability. MOFs are considered less mechani­ cally stable than zeolites due to the partial collapse of pores. Fabrication of MOFs helps enhance the stability of MOFs via the insertion of NPs and other functionalities in the MOFs. Zr-cluster-based 3,8-connected MOF in interpenetrated isomer form showed extra stability (Robison et al., 2021). Hence, fabricated MOFs have better thermal and mechanical stabilities, owing to the presence of multifunctional sites, which confer higher bond strength.
## 4.4. Surface activity of MOF sensors
The surface activity of MOF sensors is an important factor and in fabricated MOFs, the insertion of active substances inside the MOFs can assist in the growth of the active surface. The insertion of CNTs inside a Sn-MOF provides an active surface for the detection of hydrogen peroxide (Rani et al., 2020). The surface activity of Zr-trimesic acid MOF (MOF-808) for the selective detection of CA125 was enhanced via the insertion of CNTs inside the MOF structure (Biswas et al., 2021). The formation and cleavage of bonds inside MOFs could be responsible for the enhanced surface activity. For example, the insertion of TiO2 NPs and cytochrome C (CytC) in ZIF-8 MOF resulted in an active surface with Fe–S bond of CytC for better biosensing applications (Guo et al., 2020). The shape and size of pores played important roles in the fabrication process of MOFs. The fabricated materials used may be NPs or bio­ molecules, such as enzymes. The size of pores and NPs or aptamers directly affects the surface activity and sensing properties (X. Yang et al., 2021).
## 4.5. Reusability of fabricated MOFs
Fabricated MOFs can perform better as reusable materials, which is of importance in large scale applications. Reusability of MOF sensors depends on the materials attached, as well as pore shape and structure. Reusability experiments of MOFs, performed under various reaction conditions, were found to affect the void diffraction peaks. In most cases, the reusability was found to decrease after multiple uses and the recognition activities dropped dramatically with the loss of metal ions. (Zhong et al., 2019). Sometimes a reduction in the reusability of MOF sensors occurred due to mechanical damage or deactivation of func­ tional or fabricated sites. In addition, the formation of the end product can reduce the reusability of MOFs (He et al., 2016). However, MOF fabrication or immobilization with functional sites and nanoparticles have been observed to increase their reusability (Sojitra et al., 2017). The immobilization of glucoamylase in MOFs enhanced reusability up to $57\%$ for six cycles. This MOF was found to maintain $91\%$ of residual activity after several days of storage (Nadar and Rathod, 2017). The fabrication of a MOF with an enzyme enhanced the magnetic carrier properties and enabled sensing for glucose with a better reusability, with up to $84\%$ of its novel activity after 10 cycles (Z. Zhou et al., 2020). The fabrication of MOFs enhances mechanical stability, which thereby en­ hances the reusability of fabricated MOFs. However, the detachment of fabricated materials could be responsible for a drop in MOF reusable properties (Q. Li et al., 2020). The attachment of thermochromic ma­ terials to a Ni(II)-MOF sensor resulted in better reusable properties, with novel the property of exhibiting color deviation under different tem­ perature conditions (Zhang et al., 2021).
Hence, the fabricated MOFs are with the advancement of reusability. The reusability of fabricated MOFs sensors could be measured by addi­ tion and release of biomarkers to the MOFs (Fig. 10). Fabricated MOFs reusable capacity could be observed by the changes and restoration of signals (Fig. 10). However, the factors such as material compositions, immersed NPs and molecules, structural stabilities and diverse surface functionalities in the fabrication of MOFs are responsible for enhanced stability and impact biomarker detection potential. This review sum­ marizes an excellent platform for developing new fabricated MOFs, with respect to the development of cancer biomarker sensing devices.
## 5. Challenges of fabricated MOF sensors for the detection of cancer biomarkers
In this review, the various luminescence and electrochemical fabri­ cated MOF sensors have been summarized and studied. In the past few decades, observed applications of MOFs for cancer detection have been based on luminescence, electrochemical, mass spectrometry, SERS, NMR, and SEM/TEM, owing to the involvement of structural and functional diversity in signal transductions. However, in this research area, several major challenges remain to be overcome to ensure their clinical applications (Fig. 11):
1. Although various techniques and methods have been developed for the synthesis of MOFs and their derivatives with different pore sizes, it is still a big challenge to synthesize a fabricated MOF with the desired and controllable active surface, pore microenvironments, structure morphology, sizes, and topologies. These factors are highly responsible for the sensing performances of MOF sensors. It could affect the sensing capacity, rapidness and cost-effectiveness of MOF sensors.   
2. MOF hydrophilicity, optical and electrical properties are important for the development of a rapid sensor. The cancer biomarker size and structure can affect the MOF sensor sensing potential. The functional sites of MOFs are related to the detection of biomarkers. The prep­ aration of fabricated MOFs at large scale with desired selective detection of cancer biomarkers with controllable would cover the wide clinical applications.   
3. The detection of cancer biomarkers is highly determined by MOF’s surface properties. It decides the rapid detection of biomarkers at a low cost for clinical applications. Efforts have been made to develop different active surfaces based on various fabrication strategies. Though targeting, optimization and controlled modification of MOFs are quite necessary to improve the detection potential of fabricated MOF sensors for cancer biomarkers.   
4. MOF sensors for the accurate detection of cancer biomarkers with stage classification are a challenging issue. The accurate detection of cancer biomarkers at different stages of cancer can be helpful to develop rapid diagnostic clinical therapies. However, the accurate detection of cancer cells at different stages can be achieved by modifications in the MOF architecture.
5. MOF insitu application or direct injection to detect target cancer cells and factor related to the toxicity and biosafety is a critical issue. The presence of metal ions, ligands and other fabricated materials could cause toxicity in living bodies. They could damage the body parts or cells if injected inside the living bodies. The development of biode­ gradable and nontoxic components in MOFs may provide a better solution for the clinical applications of MOFs.
## 6. Summary and conclusions
In conclusion, MOFs have been employed for the development of clinical and medical applications. The fabrication of MOFs with the multifunctional sites, choosing ligands and metal NPs explore as precise luminescence and electrochemical sensors for cancer biomarkers. Such remarkable behavior of fabricated MOFs can be attributes their enlarged and active surfaces, porosity, composition and structures and utility in real samples. Although the excellent results of fabricated MOF sensors for cancer biomarkers in the signal transductions are achieved by the corresponding structural changes during electron and energy transfer.
To understand the signal transductions relationships between fabri­ cated MOFs and cancer biomarkers, monitoring of cancer biomarkers in real samples are the main issues. Fabricated MOFs with advancement of working at different temperature, solubility, time, $\mathrm{\tt{pH}},$ etc. are respon­ sible for developing desired portable methods for cancer biomarkers detection. However, the factors such as solubility, size and charge, and different functional groups can influence the detection process. Thus, the appropriate fabrication of MOFs, topological structures, metal cen­ ters, functional groups and NPs can ensure the potential detection of cancer biomarkers for large-scale sample analysis.
## 7. Future perspectives
Compared with the conventional methods and materials fabricated MOFs have yielded rapid and cost-effective tools for clinical and medical applications in cancer biomarkers detection. The prsence of inserted NPs and biomolecules in MOFs can further improve the signal amplification, sensitivity and selectivity. With this, MOFs can utilize as different technical pathways for detecting cancer biomarkers with improved ease of handling during clinical and medical applications. Moreover, advanced features, key factors for selective detection, possible in­ teractions, loading capacity, reusability, etc. can be used to design efficient and portable clinical devices. It will help to design and syn­ thesize fabricated MOFs at large-scale manufacture to develop medical devices for public health domain. Several MOF-based luminescent and electrochemical sensors have been reported for this purpose. However, a significant effort can be made to translate these research advancements into real world applications. Key areas of research interest for future prospective with respect to fabricated MOF materials should focus on the fabrication strategies, stability, toxicity and robustly to develop them cancer-detecting devices used in broadening the demand for public health assessment studies.
